{
  "title": "Properties and dunder methods",
  "summary": "Computed attributes, and how your objects plug into Python's syntax.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "@property",
      "body": [
        "Start with plain attributes. When you later need logic, turn one into a **property** without changing any caller, which is why Python code does not write `GetX()`/`SetX()`:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "class Temperature:",
        "    def __init__(self, celsius):",
        "        self.celsius = celsius",
        "",
        "    @property",
        "    def fahrenheit(self):            # read like an attribute",
        "        return self.celsius * 9 / 5 + 32",
        "",
        "    @fahrenheit.setter",
        "    def fahrenheit(self, value):",
        "        self.celsius = (value - 32) * 5 / 9",
        "",
        "t = Temperature(100)",
        "t.fahrenheit          # 212.0, no parentheses",
        "t.fahrenheit = 32     # calls the setter",
        "```",
        "",
        "This is C#'s `{ get; set; }` with bodies."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "class Square:",
        "    def __init__(self, side):",
        "        self.side = side",
        "    @property",
        "    def area(self):",
        "        return self.side ** 2",
        "",
        "s = Square(3)",
        "print(s.area)",
        "s.side = 5",
        "print(s.area)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "9",
        "25"
      ],
      "explain": "`area` is recomputed each time it is read, so it follows `side`."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Dunder methods",
      "body": [
        "Methods named `__like_this__` (\"dunder\" = double underscore) hook your class into Python's syntax and built-ins. They are like C#'s operator overloads and interface methods, but for everything:",
        "",
        "| You write | Python calls |",
        "|---|---|",
        "| `repr(x)`, the REPL, debugging | `x.__repr__()` |",
        "| `str(x)`, `print(x)` | `x.__str__()` (falls back to `__repr__`) |",
        "| `a == b` | `a.__eq__(b)` |",
        "| `a < b`, `sorted(...)` | `a.__lt__(b)` |",
        "| `len(x)` | `x.__len__()` |",
        "| `for i in x` | `x.__iter__()` |",
        "| `x[k]` | `x.__getitem__(k)` |",
        "| `a + b` | `a.__add__(b)` |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each operation to the dunder method it calls.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`len(box)`",
          "`__len__`"
        ],
        [
          "`a == b`",
          "`__eq__`"
        ],
        [
          "`print(x)`",
          "`__str__`"
        ],
        [
          "`for item in box`",
          "`__iter__`"
        ],
        [
          "`a < b`",
          "`__lt__`"
        ],
        [
          "`a + b`",
          "`__add__`"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "class Money:",
        "    def __init__(self, cents):",
        "        self.cents = cents",
        "    def __repr__(self):",
        "        return f\"Money({self.cents})\"",
        "    def __eq__(self, other):",
        "        return isinstance(other, Money) and self.cents == other.cents",
        "    def __add__(self, other):",
        "        return Money(self.cents + other.cents)",
        "",
        "a, b = Money(150), Money(150)",
        "print(a == b, a is b)",
        "print(a + b)",
        "print([Money(1)])"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "True False",
        "Money(300)",
        "[Money(1)]"
      ],
      "explain": "`==` uses your `__eq__`; `is` still checks identity. `print` and lists display objects with `__repr__`."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Without defining `__eq__`, what does `Point(1, 2) == Point(1, 2)` give?",
      "options": [
        "`False`: the default compares identity",
        "`True`: attributes are compared",
        "`TypeError`",
        "`None`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Like a C# class (not a record), the default equality is reference equality. Data classes (next lesson) write `__eq__` for you."
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Make the playlist work with `len()` and `for`.",
      "code": [
        "class Playlist:",
        "    def __init__(self, songs):",
        "        self.songs = list(songs)",
        "    def __1__(self):",
        "        return len(self.songs)",
        "    def __2__(self):",
        "        return iter(self.songs)"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "__len__",
        "__iter__"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "__count__",
        "__next__",
        "len",
        "__list__"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Vector",
      "prompt": [
        "Write a class `Vector(x, y)` with:",
        "",
        "- `__repr__` returning `Vector(1, 2)`",
        "- `__eq__` comparing both coordinates",
        "- `__add__` and `__sub__` returning new vectors",
        "- `__mul__` with a number: `Vector(1, 2) * 3` is `Vector(3, 6)`",
        "- a read-only property `length` (the Euclidean length, `(x*x + y*y) ** 0.5`)"
      ],
      "starter": "class Vector:\n    ...\n",
      "solution": [
        "class Vector:",
        "    def __init__(self, x, y):",
        "        self.x = x",
        "        self.y = y",
        "",
        "    def __repr__(self):",
        "        return f\"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})\"",
        "",
        "    def __eq__(self, other):",
        "        return isinstance(other, Vector) and (self.x, self.y) == (other.x, other.y)",
        "",
        "    def __add__(self, other):",
        "        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)",
        "",
        "    def __sub__(self, other):",
        "        return Vector(self.x - other.x, self.y - other.y)",
        "",
        "    def __mul__(self, k):",
        "        return Vector(self.x * k, self.y * k)",
        "",
        "    @property",
        "    def length(self):",
        "        return (self.x * self.x + self.y * self.y) ** 0.5"
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "Dojo.eq(\"repr\", \"Vector(1, 2)\", repr(Vector(1, 2)))",
        "Dojo.check(\"equal vectors are ==\", Vector(1, 2) == Vector(1, 2), \"define __eq__\")",
        "Dojo.check(\"different vectors are !=\", Vector(1, 2) != Vector(2, 1), \"compare both coordinates\")",
        "Dojo.eq(\"addition\", Vector(4, 6), Vector(1, 2) + Vector(3, 4))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"subtraction\", Vector(-2, -2), Vector(1, 2) - Vector(3, 4))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"scaling\", Vector(3, 6), Vector(1, 2) * 3)",
        "Dojo.eq(\"length of (3, 4)\", 5.0, Vector(3, 4).length)",
        "def dojo_set():",
        "    Vector(3, 4).length = 1",
        "Dojo.throws(\"length is read-only\", AttributeError, dojo_set)"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`__eq__(self, other)` should return `False` for non-vectors: `isinstance(other, Vector) and ...`.",
        "A `@property` without a setter is read-only automatically."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
